<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Seen in behavioral health consultation at the request of Elena Marquez, MD. [S]</paragraph><list><item>Alcohol use and trauma symptoms impair sleep, relationships, and reliability. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Gregory Boyington was born December 4, 1912, in Coeur d’Alene, Idaho. [D]</item><item>Boyington commanded Marine Fighting Squadron 214 and received the Medal of Honor. [D]</item><item>Boyington was shot down January 3, 1944, and spent approximately 20 months as a Japanese prisoner of war. [D]</item><item>Marine Corps reporting describes Boyington as beaten, interrogated, and nearly starved during captivity. [D]</item><item>A military scholarly review of a researched biography documents longstanding alcohol-related impairment, periods of sobriety, and later relapses. [D]</item><item>Post-traumatic stress disorder is a retrospective interpretation of Boyington’s combat and captivity history, not a documented lifetime diagnosis. [R]</item><item>The roster’s phrase ‘eventual sobriety’ overstates a recurrent course that included both sustained sober periods and relapse. [R]</item></list><paragraph>1 later historical claim is omitted because it describes events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

